WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
...that you at home are with us.

2
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.400
But now we're off to Lund.

3
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:06.400
More and more restaurants are using

4
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.320
AI-generated images of
dishes on their menus.

5
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.760
Isn't that right, Joel Sund?

6
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.300
Many people are quite critical
of these AI-generated menus.

7
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.640
What does it look like?

8
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.480
Looking into this, I've seen
examples from all around the world.

9
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.940
In Sweden, in most cities,
but also in Los Angeles,

10
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.360
New York, South Africa, the
Philippines. This is something

11
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.600
happening right now that
has spread from the digital

12
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.880
world to the physical world, into society.

13
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.100
On restaurant menus and
billboards you see these

14
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.900
AI-generated pictures of food,
and it stirs up a lot of feelings.

15
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.740
Online, people are saying it's
a crime against humanity.

16
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.940
They say it's disgusting, that it looks
plasticky, that it doesn't look real.

17
00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.280
Many say it's not appetizing.

18
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.120
So there's a huge debate
going on about this.

19
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.220
A lot of people don't
like this development.

20
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.400
But this is about images,

21
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.100
it's about communication, and I have
an expert on the subject with me.

22
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.600
Nils Holmberg, a communication
researcher at Lund University.

23
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.740
You know much more about this.

24
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.940
Could you tell us why this
development is happening right now?

25
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.800
Yes, it's a really exciting development,
and I think we're seeing this

26
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.400
right now because it throws us straight
into this new era of generative AI.

27
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.980
We can now create text
and images in a completely

28
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.800
new way that wasn't possible
maybe two years ago.

29
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.120
I think a great many people
in different industries,

30
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.720
different businesses, want to try this
out and see if they can save some

31
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.360
money, perhaps, by generating
professional images instead of taking

32
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.560
professional pictures with
photographers and so on.

33
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.100
And see if they can get away with
it, whether consumers react to it.

34
00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.700
In some cases I think
AI-generated images might work.

35
00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.680
In other cases, perhaps not so well.

36
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:09.780
Why do so many react so negatively
to AI-generated images of food?

37
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.360
That's also really interesting.

38
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.160
Pictures of food are a
special genre of images.

39
00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.020
There are different explanations for this.

40
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.400
One explanation is that
pictures of food actually work.

41
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:30.500
When we see a colourful picture of
a hamburger or other fast food,

42
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:35.600
we might get hungry, so it
affects us physiologically.

43
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.940
The fact that it works
also means that there are

44
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:45.420
certain rules for how
these pictures may look.

45
00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:47.420
They mustn't promise too much,

46
00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:52.560
because then we might be disappointed
by the food we're served later.

47
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.780
But neither may they in any way

48
00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.880
be unnatural or have small...

49
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.420
small differences from the real image.

50
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.660
There's an evolutionary
explanation for this.

51
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:15.640
Over a long time, humans have evolved

52
00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.520
to learn to see and recognize

53
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.980
small flaws in food, because

54
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:26.180
our lives quite simply depend on it.

55
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.980
If we eat bad food, we die.

56
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.660
This has been learned
over millions of years.

57
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.960
When we see AI-generated food,

58
00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.960
and maybe there's something
wrong with the texture, the

59
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.780
lighting, or small inaccuracies in

60
00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.080
the colours, we react strongly to it.

61
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.940
It triggers the response that
something is wrong with the food.

62
00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.100
What happens going forward?

63
00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.380
Will we change how we
see AI-generated images?

64
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.260
I think we will change in our

65
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.080
relationship to AI-generated food images.

66
00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:16.180
I think we'll become more used to

67
00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.960
AI-generated images and
synthetic media in general.

68
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:25.820
So one conceivable development
is that we'll become more

69
00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.740
accepting as we understand
how the technology works.

70
00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.380
Another possible direction is
that food images in particular

71
00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.540
may be something where we will continue

72
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:43.000
to want real pictures, where it simply

73
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.720
won't do to use AI-generated images.

74
00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.240
But AI-generated images as a
whole, I think, are here to

75
00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.240
stay in lots of other
industries and in marketing.

76
00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.180
But perhaps more doubtful
when it comes to food images.

77
00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.720
If you look online at the big debates,

78
00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.500
where people are very
harsh about these images,

79
00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.720
they talk about the uncanny valley.

80
00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.720
How does that come into play here?

81
00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.120
It's an exciting concept, and
it's used a lot in robotics

82
00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.620
and AI research, where it has been
shown that if you can build a

83
00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.040
robot that is fairly similar
to a human, then human

84
00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:27.920
users' responses mean that you get

85
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.620
more trust in that robot.

86
00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.380
This works rather...

87
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:45.200
It works like this: the more
similar something becomes, the

88
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.000
more familiar it feels and
the more trust we have.

89
00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.660
Up to a certain limit – when it gets
too similar and there are only tiny,

90
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:56.020
tiny differences, we
suddenly get a very strong,

91
00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:01.160
uncanny feeling of unease
towards the stimulus.

92
00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.360
Thank you so much, Nils Holmberg.

93
00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.720
I'm coming back into the
picture to say thanks.

94
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.060
Jovan, what do you think
of the AI food pictures?

95
00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.820
Do they make you hungry or not?

96
00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.540
Thanks, Joel.

97
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.760
Unfortunately, I don't
think they make me hungry.

98
00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:22.140
I'd rather have real pictures and
see what I'll get on my plate.

99
00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:25.460
From AI to reality, and to Janicke.

100
00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.020
We're going to talk about
this week's weather.

